her religious license you've had a  Tyrian license some educational license  and just doesn't make any sense at all  that's from the US for the USD that's  right these these these prohibitions  these sanctions are not on Cuba  they're on Americans and that's what  makes it so  and we have all this talk today about  Americans if the Republican Party is  becoming more isolationist because a lot  of Republicans don't want to intervene  in Libya and I always say who's  isolationist here who wants to prohibit  Americans from traveling to Cuba for  crying out loud  but you know if we lifted our travel  restrictions on Americans traveling to  Cuba  the next day I'm quite confident that  the Castro regime would impose some of  their own restrictions because they want  the revenue they don't want the  influence that comes with travel but if  somebody's going to limit my travel it  should be a communist knot not my own  government and that's that's how I feel  I other stuff that happened in the  during the long period of Republican  ascendancy was the Patriot Act right  where we have you know we've gone  through many different sunset revisions  over and over and everything seems to  keep coming back do we have any hope of  whittling back USA PATRIOT in a serious  way well we have in some way so we when  when the initial proposal came from the  Bush administration myself Ron Paul  Bobby Scott Bob Barr a lot of us sat  down and said you know what do we really  need and what can we take away and of  some 50 some proposals we whittled it  down to less than 20 and so we made some  headway from the beginning and then the  provisions that were troublesome we  sunset it and so we revisited them a few  times since I still have misgivings  about some of the things related to the  Patriot Act the National Security  Letters for example that just allow  government to get information from  businesses without the required warrants  I had trouble with the library provision  for example and access to public records  that individuals get and so I inserted  language or added amendments during the  last sunset that moved that up the chain  that would require the director of the  FBI to sign-off personally on these  things and so we've been able to attach  some some amendments and some protection  as we've gone along and we've been able  to sunset these provisions and we  revisit them it's kind of a it's an  uneasy balance as you go along and when  you meet with the intelligence community  they'll certainly tell you that these  provisions some of these have been very  useful and have maybe kept us safe in  certain situations and it's difficult to  talk about some of those publicly but  but it will always be a balancing act as  we go along balancing civil liberties  with the necessary tools that they need  but I've been convinced over the years  that the administration whether it's  Republican or Democrat will always seek  the maximum a number of tools and it's  up to Congress to go in and ensure that  civil liberties are protected and we've  tried and perhaps we can do a little  better job  I got a prior a little bit when you're  meeting with security types and they're  you know making the case for what how  such and such you know unraveled  whatever how much of that is backed up  by evidence and how much is take our  word for it now you have some of both  but but some of it can be backed up some  of it for example you know on these  library provisions and other things you  can actually ask them how many times was  this power of this Authority that you  have invoked and they have to tell you  and I mean there's always a possibility  that they don't tell you everything that  they're required to but I guess you just  have to assume that they will but on  some things you don't know if simply the  deterrent value of the ability to  attract all mobile devices or to have  roving wiretap or other things keeps  people who would do us harm from doing  so and that's 